+# Specialty-Side Reads Invert the Vet–Specialty Association in Memory
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The specialty directory (`REQ-VET-003`) asks the inverse of the question the veterinarian list answers: for each specialty, which veterinarians hold it. The mapping in the code runs the other way. `Vet` owns the `vet_specialties` join table through an eager `@ManyToMany`, `Specialty` carries no reference back, and no repository exists for `Specialty` at all. A specialty no veterinarian holds is unreachable from the veterinarian graph, yet the requirement lists it.
+
+The directory must also present a stable order, and the same order on every supported database vendor. The vendor schemas already diverge on collation — PostgreSQL matches owner last names case-sensitively where H2 and MySQL do not ([system-design.md § Known Defects](../system-design.md#known-defects)) — so ordering delegated to SQL would not be one order.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Map the inverse side.** Add `@ManyToMany(mappedBy = "specialties")` to `Specialty` and read the directory straight off it. Rejected: it makes a lookup value into a graph node with a cycle back to `Vet`, and `VetController`'s serialized `/vets` route marshals `Vet` including its specialties — the back-reference turns that response into a cycle. It also changes an existing persisted type's mapping for a read-only page.
+2. **A JPQL projection query joining both tables.** Rejected: the join must be an outer join to keep unheld specialties, the result needs regrouping in Java regardless, and the ordering lands in SQL where vendor collation decides it.
+3. **Read both sides and invert in memory** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+The `Vet`–`Specialty` association stays unidirectional: `Vet` holds specialties, `Specialty` holds nothing. Specialty-side reads load both collections independently — specialties through a read-only `SpecialtyRepository`, veterinarians through the existing cached `VetRepository.findAll()` — and invert them into a `SpecialtyDirectory` value object that groups veterinarians under each specialty.
+
+Ordering is applied in Java inside that value object, not in SQL: specialties by name, veterinarians by last name then first name. The rendered page is therefore identical on H2, MySQL, and PostgreSQL.
+
+`SpecialtyRepository` extends the bare `Repository` interface and exposes reads only, matching `VetRepository` rather than `PetTypeRepository`'s `JpaRepository`, so no write path to specialties exists (`NG-2`).
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The whole veterinarian and specialty set is loaded per request. Both sets are small and bounded by the clinic's staff, and the veterinarian read is already cached; this holds only while the directory stays unpaged.
+- The two reads return distinct objects for the same stored specialty, because persisted types inherit `BaseEntity`, which overrides neither `equals` nor `hashCode` and so compares by object identity. Inversion matches the two sides by stored identity (`getId()`) instead. Any later specialty-side read inherits that: object identity is not a valid match across independent reads here.
+- Grouping and ordering are unit-testable against real objects, with no database and no MVC context.
+- `/vets` serialization is unaffected, since no type gains a new reference.
+- A future specialty-side query inherits this shape. Reaching for the inverse mapping instead supersedes this ADR.
+- The vet cache has no eviction ([system-design.md § Open Questions](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey), item 5); the directory inherits that staleness exposure unchanged, and adds no cache of its own. The two sides can therefore disagree: a cached veterinarian may hold a specialty the specialty read no longer returns. The specialty side is authoritative for what the directory lists, so such a specialty is not listed and the veterinarian appears only under the specialties that are.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — the types realizing this decision
+- [2026-07-31-feature-package-organization.md](2026-07-31-feature-package-organization.md) — why these types live in the `vet` package
